#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153192} :activity/id #uuid "678f7812-7ca7-5434-8d96-4a946abcde57" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050516 :topic/id #uuid "50060817-b302-5470-841b-1327afaae882" :topic/math-academy-id 2609} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153192 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153422 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to determine if the relationship between two variables is *linear*. How would we do this?\nOne way is to draw a scatter plot and try to find a straight line that fits the data. \n\nConsider the scatter plot below that shows the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y.$ \n\n![](images/db/db07023aea39829e5567002225639ca9cc8cdcea01eb9681814eb27fe35f8eb1.png)\n\nNotice that the data points are scattered along a straight line, as shown below. This line is called the **trend line**, **regression line,** or **line of best fit.** We also say that the data has a **linear trend**.\n\n![](images/73/73a1609767711f53605a3ea8804be412596e7ff5a6295a92358ad75e3ac84631.png)\n\nOn the other hand, some data sets do *not* show a linear trend. For example, consider the following scatter plot that shows the correspondence between the variables $a$ and $b.$ \n\n![](images/66/66f80436628f69486da4cf32904a53c43f54cfcb31bfd4cba501f3cd2ab200c7.png)\n\nFrom the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear trend." :tutorial/id #uuid "3a179e75-41c8-5ad7-9406-3b20db2bbcfe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5946 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1ffbde92-cc7c-5728-b132-09515bbba591" :step/math-academy-id 36301 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153193}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153193 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152236 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152046 :question/id #uuid "dd9a05fb-f01c-5ec2-a984-9d73584065ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-9683"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "327d9a77-b2dd-57b1-b959